{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BRw3UTyVWjrynQihMgo3aG","type":"tool_result","content":"160:**One committer for main.** Close-outs never push. They hand a file set to the controller's committer, which keeps its own clean worktree of `main`, commits as `ard[bot]` with `ard(<stage>:<role>): #<issue> <summary> [skip ci]`, re-applies its snapshot on the fetched head, pushes with a lease, and retries up to 3 times (re-applying the whole-file snapshot is the rebase; if upstream changed one of its paths the request fails with a conflict instead of overwriting). A replay of an unchanged tree is a no-op that returns the upstream head. It refuses any change outside the `.ard/<issue#>/` directories. Worker code takes a separate lane: `Branches.CommitCode` commits only paths `committer.CheckCodePath` accepts (nothing under `.ard/`, `.ard-input/` or `.github/`, no `.git` or secret-shaped names), only to `worker/` branches, creating a branch with a push whose lease expects it to be absent and refusing to overwrite a path that changed on the branch since the job started. The one other lane is `ard init`'s: it commits only `.ard/config.yaml` and files under the four role trees, only to the new branch `ard-init` cut from `main`, with a push whose lease expects the ref to be absent, so it creates the branch and never updates an existing one, and opens a PR; a human merges it with a squash or merge commit, so the bot-commit check never sees a bot commit outside `.ard/<issue#>/` on main's first-parent history. Because the bot must bypass branch protection on `main`, a detective check audits it: the workflow `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` walks the first-parent history of `main` (the last 500 commits, or everything after the repository variable `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`) and fails the run, listing the commits and paths in the job summary, when a commit made by the bot changes anything but regular files under a single `.ard/<issue#>/` directory. Bot commits are identified by **committer** (the App's noreply email or login), not author, because a squash merge of a bot PR has the bot as author. If the 500-commit window is full and its oldest commit is the bot's, the check fails closed until `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE` is set, so a bad commit cannot be flushed out of view. It runs hourly as well as on push, because `[skip ci]` suppresses the push trigger. It fails closed when the `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` repository variable is unset. It reads both variables from repository settings and lives in `.github/workflows/`, which the App cannot write. Alerting is the failed run only; it does not open issues or pause ard.\n218:**The cve scanner** (`scanners/cve`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program, no build step) merges osv-scanner (all ecosystems) and govulncheck (Go reachability). Its fingerprint is `osv:<advisory>:<manifest>:<package>`, where the advisory is the smallest `GHSA-` id of the vulnerability, else the smallest `CVE-` id, else the smallest id, so one vulnerability reported under `GO-`, `GHSA-` and `CVE-` ids is one finding. Severity is `critical|high|medium|low`, from the CVSS score, else the database severity, else `medium`; a vulnerability govulncheck finds unreachable is capped at `medium`. Manifests under `testdata/`, `vendor/` and `node_modules/` are skipped. It fails closed: if a Go module cannot be analysed, the run fails and files nothing, unless a human adds a `no-govulncheck` file to the role directory, which skips govulncheck and reports reachability as unknown. Each Go module is analysed with the image's Go toolchain unless its `go` directive, or a `toolchain` line, needs a newer one. Then the scanner lets Go pick that toolchain (`GOTOOLCHAIN=auto` rules: the `toolchain` line if present, else the `go` directive's minimum version), downloads it from the Go module proxy (checksum-verified; scanner jobs have egress) into its temp dir, rebuilds the same govulncheck version with it, and scans with both. The image sets `TMPDIR=/var/tmp` because the executor's `/tmp` is noexec. If the temp dir is noexec, or the toolchain cannot be downloaded or built, the run fails closed and names the reason. Findings from such a module say which toolchain was scanned (`Go toolchain:` line), and standard-library findings are measured against it (for `go 1.27`, `go1.27.0`), not against the image's Go. The Dependabot alerts API is not wrapped, because a scanner holds no token.\n220:**The error-codes scanner** (`scanners/error-codes`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program, pattern match over source, registry and docs files) reads a human-owned `codes.json` in its role directory; nothing ships one, because error-code conventions are specific to each repository. Keys: `pattern` (required, a regex of at most 512 characters, wrapped so `E1001` does not match inside `XE10011`; it must not match the empty string), `registry` (required, a list of path patterns), `entry` (optional: a registry line must also match it to define a code), `docs` (optional; unset turns the undocumented check off), `sources` (optional, default every text file) and `exclude`. A path pattern ending in `/` is a directory prefix from the root, one without `/` matches the base name, and any other is a glob on the whole path (`*` and `?` stop at `/`, `**` does not). Unknown keys, wrong types and lists over 100 entries are errors. Codes found in registry files (on `entry` lines) are R, codes anywhere in docs files are D, codes in every other text file are S. Findings: `error-codes:duplicated:<code>` (high, group `duplicate codes`: a code on two or more distinct registry lines), `error-codes:unregistered:<code>` (medium, group `unregistered codes`: in S, not in R) and `error-codes:undocumented:<code>` (low, group `undocumented codes`: in R, not in D, only when `docs` is set). Fingerprints use the code alone, so moving a use never churns an issue. `.git`, `.ard`, `vendor`, `node_modules`, `testdata`, symlinks, binary files, files of about 1 MiB or more and lines over 16 KiB are skipped. A finding carries only the code and repo-relative `path:line` locations, never line text, and codes outside `[A-Za-z0-9_.:/+=-]{1,128}` are dropped with a stderr count. Because the scanner is `autoclose`, it fails the run rather than see less: an invalid `codes.json`, a `registry` or `docs` that matches no file, a file in both, an unreadable file, a failing tool or more than 5,000 findings all exit non-zero with no output. With no `codes.json` it logs `not configured` and succeeds with no findings, so deleting `codes.json` closes its open issues as \"No longer detected\". ard does not run it on itself until ard has error codes.\n371:- the diff touches no protected path; the list comes only from `merge.protected_paths`, with no floor in code (decided, #3), so a human who empties the list removes the gate. `ard init` proposes the defaults `.github/`, `.ard/config.yaml`, the four role trees (`.ard/scanners/`, `.ard/triage/`, `.ard/workers/`, `.ard/reviewers/`), `**/CODEOWNERS`, and the agent instructions a PR could plant to steer later agents: `CLAUDE.md`, `**/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `**/AGENTS.md`, `.claude/` and `.mcp.json` (13 entries; `**/X` already matches a root `X`, the bare entries are kept for clarity). An entry ending in `/` is a directory prefix; any other entry is a doublestar glob. Matching is case-insensitive on the repo-relative path (macOS file systems are, so `Claude.md` and `.Claude/x` are protected). A prefix matches on a directory boundary (`.github/` matches `.github` and `.github/x`, never `.githubx`). A glob follows doublestar: `*` stays within one path segment and `**` crosses them, so a bare `CODEOWNERS` would match only the root file. Quote globs that start with `*` in YAML. The merger checks both names of a renamed file; a malformed path counts as protected. A PR that touches one gets `ard:needs-approval` and merges on the next poll once a person with write access approves the current head with a GitHub review (below).\n410:merge:                       # defaults written by `ard init`; the list is the only source (no floor in code)\n411:  protected_paths: [.github/, .ard/config.yaml, .ard/scanners/, .ard/triage/, .ard/workers/, .ard/reviewers/, \"**/CODEOWNERS\", CLAUDE.md, \"**/CLAUDE.md\", AGENTS.md, \"**/AGENTS.md\", .claude/, .mcp.json]\n420:  scanners/error-codes:     { every: 24h, group_size: 20, autoclose: true }\n432:Scanners also take `route` (`pending`, `none` or `needs-human`). Anything unset takes its default: intake in `label` mode with `ard:pending`, scanners every 4 h with `max_open` 5 and `group_size` 10, triage every 15 min, a 30-minute job timeout, `make test`, 2 test-fix attempts, 3 review rounds and worker diff thresholds of 1,000 lines (warn) and 2,000 lines (hold). Durations accept `m`, `h` and `d`. A sidecar `repo` must be `owner/name` and its `ref` a valid git ref name (no `:`, leading `-`, `..` or spaces; an abbreviated SHA is rejected, use all 40 characters); `ard config check` reports the file and key. A worker's `timeout` and `max_budget_usd` cover the whole job (every step), and `tests: \"\"` disables its test step. Per-role keys are `every`, `max_open`, `group_size`, `autoclose`, `timeout`, `agent`, `model`, `max_budget_usd`, `budget_usd_per_day` (that role's own daily cap in USD; unset or 0 means only the repo cap applies), `sidecar`, `after`, `plan_section`, `tests`, `max_test_fixes`, `max_review_rounds`, `diff_warn_lines` and `diff_max_lines` (worker roles; positive integers, warn no more than max; above warn the PR proceeds with a note, above max it is held), `reviewers` and `when` (`paths`, or `triage` roles that ran; at least one is required). `ard config check` warns when every reviewer has a `when:` rule, because a PR matching none gets no review and stays a draft. `plan_section` is for triage roles: the `## <heading>` of `plan.md` the role owns (one line, no leading `#`, unique across triage roles). The starter roles default to Requirements (product-manager), Design (architect), UX (designer), Docs (documentation-planner) and Implementation (engineer), and a value in `config.yaml` overrides that; a triage role with none may write only its own role file. There are no per-role config files: `ard init` writes a role's starting overrides into `config.yaml` for the roles it ships. Once a second repo adopts ard, role directories can move to a shared roles repo pinned by ref, like a sidecar; a repo's own directory of the same name wins. `after` names triage roles, with no self-reference and no cycles.\n434:**`ard init` proposes the starter `.ard/` as a PR (decided, #27).** It builds `config.yaml` and the starter roles in memory (today `scanners/cve`, `scanners/docs`, `scanners/error-codes`, `triage/lead`, `triage/engineer`, `workers/engineer`, `reviewers/eng-review`, `reviewers/license-review` and `reviewers/security-review`; `intake: {mode: label}`, the protected-path defaults above, and the cve, docs, error-codes, lead, engineer, worker, eng-review, security-review and license-review overrides), compares them with `main`, commits them on a new branch `ard-init` cut from `main` through the effect layer and a narrow committer lane, pushes it and opens a PR. It writes nothing to the local working tree or to `main`, and `scan` is committed `100755`. Files that already exist on `main` with different contents are a conflict naming the paths; `--force` proposes overwriting them, and init never deletes anything. It refuses while `ard-init` exists on the remote, whatever its PR's state, so it never force-pushes over or stacks commits on a human's edits: merge or close the PR, delete the branch and rerun. If `main` already has every starter file it prints `nothing to propose`. It syncs the label catalogue first (like `ard labels sync`), so a failure leaves nothing pushed. With `--dry-run` the commit, push and PR are logged to the ledger instead of acting. A missing repo, credentials or `main` branch exits 1 with nothing written, and `ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN` auth is refused outside `--dry-run`. The PR body carries the one step the App cannot do: copy `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` and set the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` (optionally `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`). It also says to merge with **Squash and merge** or **Create a merge commit**, never a fast-forward from the command line, which would put the bot's commit on main's first-parent history. **Rebase and merge** is also safe: the scratch-repo run (#85) showed GitHub records the merging human as committer of a rebase-merged commit (the bot stays the author), and the bot-commit check keys on committer, so it does not flag it. The body still names only the two merges above.\n447:- **Planted repo config does not steer agents.** A branch can carry CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, `.claude/` settings, hooks, skills, agents or a `.mcp.json`. The Claude adapter's flags stop all of them loading (see Executors). Residual risk: auth isolation comes from the empty per-job config dir and HOME, not a flag (the CLI reads no Keychain entry but the one keyed to the per-job dir); a host-mode job on macOS still runs as the operator's uid, so agent Bash could call `security` against the operator's login Keychain, including Claude's own entry, which is within the host-mode risk of #84 (a Dockerfile role or a dedicated macOS user for `ard serve` closes it); the subscription token is a one-year, model-only credential, rotated by running `claude setup-token` again and restarting `ard serve`; `--max-budget-usd` and `total_cost_usd` are estimates on a subscription; part of the block (AGENTS.md, and CLAUDE.md under `--restricted`) is observed behaviour that holds only on the tested CLI version and is re-checked by the integration tests; built-in plugins and the host's managed settings still apply; system reminders and fixed CLI prompt text are back; isolation of settings `env` is not pinned by a test, since no Bash call is scripted; Bash can still read anything the process can (host mode, #84); and an agent can rewrite CLAUDE.md or `.claude/` in its worktree, which later runs ignore but a worker PR could carry into the target repo (the `ard init` protected-path defaults cover `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `.claude/` and `.mcp.json`, so such a PR needs a human).\n449:- **Role files are human-owned.** The diff guard and the committer's code lane reject any worker change under `.ard/` or `.github/` (so `.ard/config.yaml` and every role directory), and the merger treats those paths as protected through `merge.protected_paths` (the `ard init` defaults), so no agent can rewrite its own prompt. `ard init` only proposes them, in a PR a human merges.\n481:How ard itself is built, published and deployed. Jenkins and Argo CD do the work; the cluster-specific wiring (the Jenkins job definitions and their publishing Jenkinsfiles, credentials, the Argo CD projects and the image pin) lives in the deployment repository, not here. This repository holds only what is ard's: the `Makefile` targets CI runs, `Dockerfile`, `deploy/` and `deploy/cosign.pub`, the key ard images are signed with. `Dockerfile`, `.dockerignore`, `deploy/` and any `Jenkinsfile*` are in ard's `merge.protected_paths`, so the merger never lands a change to them without a human approval.\n7:ard is a controller that automates repository development: scanners → triage → worker → reviewers → merger, coordinated through `ard:` GitHub labels. The design is [docs/proposal.md](docs/proposal.md). Read the relevant section before changing behaviour.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c5eb0b10-10f7-42ca-8b7a-846dd3a3e8b7","uuid":"b93cda6f-c7de-47af-b6e5-14a7b91417fb","timestamp":"2026-11-23T21:28:10.950Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"160:**One committer for main.** Close-outs never push. They hand a file set to the controller's committer, which keeps its own clean worktree of `main`, commits as `ard[bot]` with `ard(<stage>:<role>): #<issue> <summary> [skip ci]`, re-applies its snapshot on the fetched head, pushes with a lease, and retries up to 3 times (re-applying the whole-file snapshot is the rebase; if upstream changed one of its paths the request fails with a conflict instead of overwriting). A replay of an unchanged tree is a no-op that returns the upstream head. It refuses any change outside the `.ard/<issue#>/` directories. Worker code takes a separate lane: `Branches.CommitCode` commits only paths `committer.CheckCodePath` accepts (nothing under `.ard/`, `.ard-input/` or `.github/`, no `.git` or secret-shaped names), only to `worker/` branches, creating a branch with a push whose lease expects it to be absent and refusing to overwrite a path that changed on the branch since the job started. The one other lane is `ard init`'s: it commits only `.ard/config.yaml` and files under the four role trees, only to the new branch `ard-init` cut from `main`, with a push whose lease expects the ref to be absent, so it creates the branch and never updates an existing one, and opens a PR; a human merges it with a squash or merge commit, so the bot-commit check never sees a bot commit outside `.ard/<issue#>/` on main's first-parent history. Because the bot must bypass branch protection on `main`, a detective check audits it: the workflow `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` walks the first-parent history of `main` (the last 500 commits, or everything after the repository variable `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`) and fails the run, listing the commits and paths in the job summary, when a commit made by the bot changes anything but regular files under a single `.ard/<issue#>/` directory. Bot commits are identified by **committer** (the App's noreply email or login), not author, because a squash merge of a bot PR has the bot as author. If the 500-commit window is full and its oldest commit is the bot's, the check fails closed until `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE` is set, so a bad commit cannot be flushed out of view. It runs hourly as well as on push, because `[skip ci]` suppresses the push trigger. It fails closed when the `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` repository variable is unset. It reads both variables from repository settings and lives in `.github/workflows/`, which the App cannot write. Alerting is the failed run only; it does not open issues or pause ard.\n218:**The cve scanner** (`scanners/cve`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program, no build step) merges osv-scanner (all ecosystems) and govulncheck (Go reachability). Its fingerprint is `osv:<advisory>:<manifest>:<package>`, where the advisory is the smallest `GHSA-` id of the vulnerability, else the smallest `CVE-` id, else the smallest id, so one vulnerability reported under `GO-`, `GHSA-` and `CVE-` ids is one finding. Severity is `critical|high|medium|low`, from the CVSS score, else the database severity, else `medium`; a vulnerability govulncheck finds unreachable is capped at `medium`. Manifests under `testdata/`, `vendor/` and `node_modules/` are skipped. It fails closed: if a Go module cannot be analysed, the run fails and files nothing, unless a human adds a `no-govulncheck` file to the role directory, which skips govulncheck and reports reachability as unknown. Each Go module is analysed with the image's Go toolchain unless its `go` directive, or a `toolchain` line, needs a newer one. Then the scanner lets Go pick that toolchain (`GOTOOLCHAIN=auto` rules: the `toolchain` line if present, else the `go` directive's minimum version), downloads it from the Go module proxy (checksum-verified; scanner jobs have egress) into its temp dir, rebuilds the same govulncheck version with it, and scans with both. The image sets `TMPDIR=/var/tmp` because the executor's `/tmp` is noexec. If the temp dir is noexec, or the toolchain cannot be downloaded or built, the run fails closed and names the reason. Findings from such a module say which toolchain was scanned (`Go toolchain:` line), and standard-library findings are measured against it (for `go 1.27`, `go1.27.0`), not against the image's Go. The Dependabot alerts API is not wrapped, because a scanner holds no token.\n220:**The error-codes scanner** (`scanners/error-codes`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program, pattern match over source, registry and docs files) reads a human-owned `codes.json` in its role directory; nothing ships one, because error-code conventions are specific to each repository. Keys: `pattern` (required, a regex of at most 512 characters, wrapped so `E1001` does not match inside `XE10011`; it must not match the empty string), `registry` (required, a list of path patterns), `entry` (optional: a registry line must also match it to define a code), `docs` (optional; unset turns the undocumented check off), `sources` (optional, default every text file) and `exclude`. A path pattern ending in `/` is a directory prefix from the root, one without `/` matches the base name, and any other is a glob on the whole path (`*` and `?` stop at `/`, `**` does not). Unknown keys, wrong types and lists over 100 entries are errors. Codes found in registry files (on `entry` lines) are R, codes anywhere in docs files are D, codes in every other text file are S. Findings: `error-codes:duplicated:<code>` (high, group `duplicate codes`: a code on two or more distinct registry lines), `error-codes:unregistered:<code>` (medium, group `unregistered codes`: in S, not in R) and `error-codes:undocumented:<code>` (low, group `undocumented codes`: in R, not in D, only when `docs` is set). Fingerprints use the code alone, so moving a use never churns an issue. `.git`, `.ard`, `vendor`, `node_modules`, `testdata`, symlinks, binary files, files of about 1 MiB or more and lines over 16 KiB are skipped. A finding carries only the code and repo-relative `path:line` locations, never line text, and codes outside `[A-Za-z0-9_.:/+=-]{1,128}` are dropped with a stderr count. Because the scanner is `autoclose`, it fails the run rather than see less: an invalid `codes.json`, a `registry` or `docs` that matches no file, a file in both, an unreadable file, a failing tool or more than 5,000 findings all exit non-zero with no output. With no `codes.json` it logs `not configured` and succeeds with no findings, so deleting `codes.json` closes its open issues as \"No longer detected\". ard does not run it on itself until ard has error codes.\n371:- the diff touches no protected path; the list comes only from `merge.protected_paths`, with no floor in code (decided, #3), so a human who empties the list removes the gate. `ard init` proposes the defaults `.github/`, `.ard/config.yaml`, the four role trees (`.ard/scanners/`, `.ard/triage/`, `.ard/workers/`, `.ard/reviewers/`), `**/CODEOWNERS`, and the agent instructions a PR could plant to steer later agents: `CLAUDE.md`, `**/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `**/AGENTS.md`, `.claude/` and `.mcp.json` (13 entries; `**/X` already matches a root `X`, the bare entries are kept for clarity). An entry ending in `/` is a directory prefix; any other entry is a doublestar glob. Matching is case-insensitive on the repo-relative path (macOS file systems are, so `Claude.md` and `.Claude/x` are protected). A prefix matches on a directory boundary (`.github/` matches `.github` and `.github/x`, never `.githubx`). A glob follows doublestar: `*` stays within one path segment and `**` crosses them, so a bare `CODEOWNERS` would match only the root file. Quote globs that start with `*` in YAML. The merger checks both names of a renamed file; a malformed path counts as protected. A PR that touches one gets `ard:needs-approval` and merges on the next poll once a person with write access approves the current head with a GitHub review (below).\n410:merge:                       # defaults written by `ard init`; the list is the only source (no floor in code)\n411:  protected_paths: [.github/, .ard/config.yaml, .ard/scanners/, .ard/triage/, .ard/workers/, .ard/reviewers/, \"**/CODEOWNERS\", CLAUDE.md, \"**/CLAUDE.md\", AGENTS.md, \"**/AGE